---
/* DRAFT - must be reviewed by a licensed US attorney before publishing. */
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { site, addressLines } from '../config/site';

const { legal } = site;

const toc = [
	{ id: 'summary', title: 'The short version' },
	{ id: 'fixed-scope', title: 'Fixed-scope projects' },
	{ id: 'cancellation-window', title: 'Cancelling before work starts' },
	{ id: 'non-refundable', title: 'What is never refundable' },
	{ id: 'hourly-retainer', title: 'Hourly and retainer work' },
	{ id: 'recurring-plans', title: 'Recurring maintenance and hosting plans' },
	{ id: 'our-cancellation', title: 'If we cancel' },
	{ id: 'requesting-a-refund', title: 'How to request a refund' },
	{ id: 'chargebacks', title: 'Chargebacks' },
	{ id: 'your-rights', title: 'Your statutory rights' },
	{ id: 'contact', title: 'How to reach us' },
];
---

<LegalLayout
	title="Refund Policy"
	intro="This page says plainly what happens to your money if a project changes, is cancelled, or goes wrong. Where an SOW states different terms, the SOW controls for that project."
	lastUpdated={legal.effectiveDate}
	toc={toc}
	seo={{
		title: 'Refund Policy',
		description:
			'How Linkly LLC handles deposits, milestone refunds, cancellations, retainer billing and auto-renewing maintenance plans, and how to request a refund.',
	}}
>
	<h2 id="summary">The short version</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			A project deposit is <strong>non-refundable</strong>. It pays for
			discovery work and reserves your slot in our schedule.
		</li>
		<li>
			You can cancel for a <strong>full refund before any work begins</strong>,
			within the cancellation window below.
		</li>
		<li>
			For a fixed-scope project you are refunded for <strong
				>milestones that have not been started</strong
			>. Milestones already delivered are not refunded.
		</li>
		<li>
			For hourly or retainer work there is <strong
				>no refund for hours already worked</strong
			>, but you can stop with the notice period below.
		</li>
		<li>
			Recurring plans can be cancelled <strong
				>in the same way you signed up</strong
			>, and we will remind you before each renewal.
		</li>
		<li>
			Third-party costs we have already paid — domains, licences, hosting — are <strong
				>never refunded</strong
			>, because we cannot get them back.
		</li>
		<li>
			We do everything ourselves, quickly, and we do not charge a fee for a
			refund we agree is warranted.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="fixed-scope">Fixed-scope projects</h2>
	<h3>Deposits</h3>
	<p>
		Most projects start with a deposit of
		<strong>[PLACEHOLDER: 30-50% of the project fee]</strong>. That deposit is
		non-refundable. It exists because it pays for two real costs: the discovery
		and planning work that happens before you see anything, and the reservation
		of delivery capacity. Once we have turned that capacity down for another
		client, we cannot simply hand it back.
	</p>

	<h3>Milestone billing</h3>
	<p>
		Fixed-scope projects are broken into milestones. Each milestone has a
		written definition of done in the SOW. We invoice a milestone when it is
		delivered, not when it is started, unless the SOW says otherwise.
	</p>
	<p>The rules that follow are the default position:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Not started</strong> — a milestone you cancel before we begin is <strong
				>refunded in full</strong
			>, or credited against a replacement scope at your choice.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>In progress</strong> — a partially delivered milestone is settled at
			<strong
				>[PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "the percentage of the milestone value that reflects
				completed work"]</strong
			>. Anything above that is refunded.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Delivered and accepted</strong> — <strong>not refundable</strong>.
			The work has been done and you have the benefit of it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Delivered, not yet accepted</strong> — where a milestone includes a
			review period stated in the SOW, work is treated as accepted when you approve
			it or when the review period ends without objection.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="cancellation-window">Cancelling before work starts</h2>
	<p>
		If you cancel a project <strong>before any work has begun</strong> — meaning no
		discovery, planning or build work has been started — we issue a
		<strong>full refund within [PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "7 days"]</strong> of receiving
		your cancellation notice, and we release your scheduled slot.
	</p>
	<p>
		This window exists so that the question is answered clearly and early. Once
		discovery has started, the deposit has been earned and the project has
		consumed capacity, so the terms above for milestone settlement apply
		instead.
	</p>
	<p>
		To cancel within the window, reply to your proposal or SOW email with the
		words "cancel project" and the project name. No justification is required
		and no negotiation is needed to receive the refund.
	</p>

	<h2 id="non-refundable">What is never refundable</h2>
	<p>
		The following are not refundable, because the work is done or the cost is
		already spent:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Work that has been completed and delivered</strong>, including
			completed milestones, completed hours, and delivered documentation.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Work delivered but not yet accepted</strong>, once the review
			period in the SOW has passed without objection.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Third-party costs already incurred</strong>, including domain
			registrations, SSL certificates, hosting and infrastructure set-up,
			software and plugin licences, API and model usage, app store fees, and
			third-party services. We can usually show you the invoice.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Custom work created specifically for you</strong> that cannot be reused,
			where a licence is issued to you — the licence stays with you, so the value
			is delivered even on a cancellation.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Discovery and planning work</strong> already performed, including audits,
			technical research, wireframes, and the written scope itself.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Time spent on a cancelled or paused project</strong> before an agreed
			notice period expires, where we have turned down other work to hold your capacity.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		We are not required to refund work that was never requested or that falls
		outside the agreed scope. If you would like work outside the scope started,
		ask first.
	</p>

	<h2 id="hourly-retainer">Hourly and retainer work</h2>
	<p>
		Hourly and retainer work is billed for time actually worked at the agreed
		rate. We do not refund hours already worked. Time is recorded against your
		project and is available to you on request.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Retainer plans</strong> renew for the term stated in your agreement.
			To stop, give <strong
				>[PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "30 days' written notice"]</strong
			> before the renewal date. Work continues during the notice period, and you
			keep access to everything delivered to that date.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Unused hours</strong> in a monthly retainer do not roll over unless
			the agreement says so, and do not carry a cash value.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Overage hours</strong> are only worked if you approve them in advance
			or the agreement authorises them up to a stated cap.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Where retainer work is paused or ended, we provide a handover: source code,
		documentation, credentials and access under your accounts, so you are not
		dependent on us.
	</p>

	<h2 id="recurring-plans">Recurring maintenance and hosting plans</h2>
	<div class="legal-callout">
		<p>
			<strong
				>These plans renew automatically. If you want to stop, you must cancel —
				not simply stop paying.</strong
			> Cancelling is done in the same way you signed up, using the cancellation link
			in your welcome or renewal email, or by replying to any plan email. There is
			no account manager to call and no retention process to get through.
		</p>
	</div>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Auto-renewal.</strong> A maintenance or hosting plan continues at the
			same price and term until cancelled. We will send a reminder <strong
				>at least [PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "7 days"] before each renewal</strong
			>, and at least <strong
				>[PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "30 days"] before renewal where state law requires
				longer</strong
			>. [PLACEHOLDER: attorney to confirm the notice period required by
			California's Automatic Renewal Law and by state auto-renewal statutes
			generally]
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>How to cancel.</strong> Use the cancellation link in the email, or reply
			to any email from the plan address. You will receive a cancellation confirmation.
			Cancelling is as easy as signing up.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Effect of cancellation.</strong> The plan stays active until the end
			of the period you have already paid for. After that, hosting and maintenance
			stop. We will tell you what happens to your data and give you at least <strong
				>[PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "30 days"]</strong
			> to retrieve it, after which we may delete it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>No refund for a used period.</strong> A plan is not refunded for the
			remainder of a period you have already used. If you cancel immediately after
			a renewal, the renewed amount is refundable on a pro-rata basis for the unused
			portion, less any pass-through costs already incurred.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="our-cancellation">If we cancel</h2>
	<p>
		If <strong>we</strong> cancel a project or discontinue a plan without cause, the
		position is the mirror image of yours:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			You receive a <strong
				>pro-rata refund of all fees paid for work not yet delivered</strong
			>, calculated on the same pro-rata basis set out above.
		</li>
		<li>
			You receive <strong>everything produced to date</strong> — source code, designs,
			documentation, infrastructure configuration — whether or not you have paid in
			full for it, so you are not left with nothing.
		</li>
		<li>
			We complete a handover of accounts, credentials and documentation, and
			cooperate for <strong>[PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "30 days"]</strong> in reasonable transition
			assistance, charged at our standard rate and only if you ask for it.
		</li>
		<li>
			We will give you <strong>[PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "14 days"]</strong> written notice
			where we can.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		If we cancel because of an unpaid invoice, after the notice and cure period
		in our
		<a href="/terms-of-service">Terms of Service</a>, the pro-rata refund above
		does not apply and the deposit is non-refundable.
	</p>

	<h2 id="requesting-a-refund">How to request a refund</h2>
	<p>
		Email <a href={`mailto:${legal.contactEmail}`}>{legal.contactEmail}</a> with the
		subject line <strong>"Refund request"</strong> and include your name, the project
		or plan name, and the invoice or transaction reference. You do not need to explain
		or justify the request for us to review it.
	</p>
	<p>We commit to the following:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong
				>Acknowledgement within [PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "3 business days"].</strong
			>
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>A decision within [PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "10 business days"]</strong> of
			receiving the request.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Payment within [PLACEHOLDER: e.g. "10 business days"]</strong> of approval.
		</li>
		<li>
			Refunds are issued <strong>to the original payment method</strong>. We
			cannot refund to a different account or method. Refund processing times
			are set by your bank or card issuer and are typically 5 to 10 business
			days after we send the refund.
		</li>
		<li>
			If we decline a request, we tell you why and give you a real route to
			challenge it — reply to the same email, or use the dispute process below.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		This process is in addition to any right you have under the
		<a href="/terms-of-service">Terms of Service</a> or under applicable law. Nothing
		here limits those rights.
	</p>

	<h2 id="chargebacks">Chargebacks</h2>
	<div class="legal-callout">
		<p>
			<strong
				>Please contact us before you dispute a payment with your bank or card
				issuer.</strong
			> A chargeback costs both of us real money and adds days to resolving something
			we can usually settle in a week. Email us with the transaction reference and
			we will prioritise it.
		</p>
	</div>
	<p>
		If you do open a chargeback, we will still apply the refund process in full
		and in addition. Where you dispute a charge and we are ultimately found not
		liable for it, we may pass on the chargeback fee to you.
	</p>

	<h2 id="your-rights">Your statutory rights</h2>
	<p>
		Nothing in this policy, and nothing in our
		<a href="/terms-of-service">Terms of Service</a>, limits your rights under
		applicable state or federal law. This includes, without limitation, the
		right to a refund required by state law, the protections of the FTC's
		Magnets-Moxie Rule, any California consumer protection rights, and any right
		of return or cancellation you have as a consumer.
	</p>
	<p>
		Where a state law gives you a right that is more favourable to you than this
		policy, that law controls, and this policy does not waive it. [PLACEHOLDER:
		attorney to confirm which state-specific refund rights apply to Linkly LLC's
		clients]
	</p>

	<h2 id="contact">How to reach us</h2>
	<p>
		{legal.entityName}<br />
		{
			addressLines.map(line => (
				<>
					{line}
					<br />
				</>
			))
		}
		Email: <a href={`mailto:${legal.contactEmail}`}>{legal.contactEmail}</a>
	</p>
</LegalLayout>

<style>
	.legal-callout {
		margin: 1.5rem 0;
		padding: 1.15rem 1.35rem;
		border-left: 4px solid var(--color-brand-ink);
		border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius-sm) var(--border-radius-sm) 0;
		background: var(--theme-surface-1);
	}

	.legal-callout p {
		margin: 0;
	}
</style>
